#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192585 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93dcc73c-ce2d-5fa3-b32b-9fc600fb269e" :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the First Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "c700cc22-203a-5ad3-913c-e5eb507e22e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13995" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n${a + 4b = 1; 2a + b =-5$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $a$ from this system by filling in the missing information below.\n\n$\\qquad$ *To eliminate $a$, multiply the $\\,\\textbf{__________}\\,$equation by $\\,\\textbf{______}\\,$,\nand add the equations.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is $1$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-2}$, then the coefficient of $a$ will turn into $-2$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n{(a + 4b = 1)\\cdot (-2); 2a + b =-5\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2a - 8b &= -2 \\\\\n2a + b &= -5 \\\\\n(-8b + b) &= (-2 - 5) \\\\\n- 7b &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $a$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2}$, and add the equations*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192586 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e7bfcbe-4913-53e0-98c7-171965adfe62" :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "eb7f1f32-1a5c-526e-878b-8046c4feb8c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13997" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n${3x + 4y = 2; x - 2y = 4$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from this system by filling in the missing information below.\n\n$\\qquad$ *To eliminate $y$, multiply the $\\,\\textbf{__________}\\,$equation by $\\,\\textbf{______}\\,$,\nand add the equations.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $4$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}2}$, then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $-4$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n{(3x + 4y = 2; (x - 2y = 4)\\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 4y &= 2 \\\\\n2x - 4y &= 8 \\\\\n(3x + 2x) &= (2 + 8) \\\\\n5x &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $y$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$, and add the equations*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192587 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dbf4e1a-2c87-562d-8c4d-41fd32e79b86" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the First Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "f20f46d9-b799-57a2-90bc-12e6593e8655" :question/math-academy-id "e-13999" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n${2x + 3y = 8; x - 2y =-3$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $2$, while in the second equation it is $1$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then the $x$-coefficient will become $-2$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n{(2x + 3y = 8; (x - 2y =-3) \\cdot (-2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 3y &= 8 \\\\\n- 2x + 4y &= 6 \\\\\n(3y + 4y) &= (8 + 6) \\\\\n7y &= 14 \\\\\ny &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = 2$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y = 2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= -3 \\\\\nx - 2(2) &= -3 \\\\\nx - 4 &= -3 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=1, \\,y=2$, which can be expressed as $(1,2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192588 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "990e3660-dfd7-50fa-b60b-0478ff02a073" :question/math-academy-id "e-14002" :question/problem "Find the solution $(u,v)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n${5u - 6v = 3; - 3u + 2v =-5$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $v$-coefficient in the first equation is $-6$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $3$, then the $v$-coefficient will become $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n{5u - 6v = 3; (-3u + 2v =-5) \\cdot (3)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $v$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5u - 6v &= 3 \\\\\n- 9u + 6v &= -15 \\\\\n(5u - 9u) &= (3 - 15) \\\\\n- 4u &= -12 \\\\\nu &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $u = 3$. However, we still need to find $v$. To do this, we substitute $u = 3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3u + 2v &= -5 \\\\\n- 3(3) + 2v &= -5 \\\\\n- 9 + 2v &= -5 \\\\\n2v &= 4 \\\\\nv &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nu = 3, \\,v = 2\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(3,2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dbf4e1a-2c87-562d-8c4d-41fd32e79b86" :knowledge-point/ti